        <h1>Moral and Ethical Alignment</h1>

        <p>Moral and ethical alignment is a system used to categorize characters and creatures based on their ethical and moral perspectives. It is often represented as a two-dimensional grid with nine distinct alignments, combining the axes of "Lawful vs. Chaotic" and "Good vs. Evil."</p>

<p>Monsters Masters & Mobsters recognizes the <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alignment_%28role-playing_games%29">alignment scale</a> used in <cite>Dungeons and Dragons</cite> and other role-playing games. <a href="/lore/anthropes">Anthrope</a>s and <a href="/lore/organizations">Organization</a>s can have alignment. <a href="/lore/monsters">Monster</a>s do not have alignment.

<p>In the D&D alignment systemn, there are two axes of alignment: a "moral" continuum between good and evil, an "ethical" continuum between law and chaos. The nine alignments are combinations of these two axes.</p>

<h2>Good vs. Evil</h2>

<p>Good anthropes and organizations protect innocent life. Evil anthropes and organizations debase or destroy innocent life, whether for fun or profit.</p>

<p>"Good" implies altruism, respect for life, and a concern for the dignity of anthropes and monsters. Good anthropes and organizations sacrifice their energy and resources as needed to help others.</p>
    
<p>"Evil" implies hurting, oppressing, and killing others. Some evil anthropes and organizations simply have no compassion for others and kill without qualms if doing so is convenient. Others actively pursue evil, killing for sport or out of duty to some evil master.</p>

<p>Anthropes and organizations who are neutral with respect to good and evil have compunctions against killing the innocent but lack the commitment to make sacrifices to protect or help others. Neutral anthropes and organizations are committed to others by personal relationships.</p>

<p>Being good or evil can be a conscious choice. For most anthropes and organizations, though, being good or evil is an attitude that one recognizes but does not choose. Being neutral on the good-evil axis usually represents a lack of commitment one way or the other, but for some it represents a positive commitment to a balanced view. While acknowledging that good and evil are objective states, not just opinions, these anthropes and organizations maintain that a balance between the two is the proper place for people, or at least for them.</p>

<p>Monsters are incapable of moral action and thus are neutral rather than good or evil. An untrained monster will act based on the threat it perceives to its life or the life of its offspring. A properly-trained monster will act at the command of its master without comprehension of the morality of the command.</p>

<h2>Law vs. Chaos</h2>

<p>Lawful anthropes and organizations believe in order, structure, and predictability. Chaotic anthropes and organizations believe in freedom, adaptability, and spontaneity.</p>

<p>Lawful anthropes and organizations tell the truth, keep their word, respect authority, honor tradition, and judge those who fall short of their duties.</p>

<p>Chaotic anthropes and organizations follow their consciences, resent being told what to do, favor new ideas over tradition, and do what they promise if they feel like it.</p>

<p>"Law" implies honor, trustworthiness, obedience to authority, and reliability. On the downside, lawfulness can include close-mindedness, reactionary adherence to tradition, judgmentalness, and a lack of adaptability. Those anthropes and organizations who consciously promote lawfulness say that only lawful behavior creates a society in which people can depend on each other and make the right decisions in full confidence that others will act as they should.</p>

<p>"Chaos" implies freedom, adaptability, and flexibility. On the downside, chaos can include recklessness, resentment toward legitimate authority, arbitrary actions, and irresponsibility. Those anthropes and organizations who promote chaotic behavior say that only unfettered personal freedom allows people to express themselves fully and lets society benefit from the potential that its individuals have within them.</p>

<p>Someone who is neutral with respect to law and chaos has a normal respect for authority and feels neither a compulsion to obey nor a compulsion to rebel. She is honest but can be tempted into lying or deceiving others.</p>

<p>Devotion to law or chaos may be a conscious choice, but more often it is a personality trait that is recognized rather than being chosen. Neutrality on the lawful-chaotic axis is usually simply a middle state, a state of not feeling compelled toward one side or the other. Some few such neutrals, however, espouse neutrality as superior to law or chaos, regarding each as an extreme with its own blind spots and drawbacks.</p>

<p>Organizations imbed lawfulness or chaos into their structures. A lawful organization has clear rules, a chain of command, and established procedures. A chaotic organization has few rules, a flexible chain of command, and adaptable procedures.</p>

<p>Just as monsters are neutral to moral alignment, they are neutral to legal alignment. A properly-trained monster will act at the command of its master without comprehension of whether the command violates the law of anything that has jurisdiction over it.</p>

<h2>The Nine Alignments</h2>

<p>Nine distinct alignments define all the possible combinations of the lawful-chaotic axis with the good-evil axis. Each alignment description below depicts a typical anthrope or organization of that alignment. Remember that individuals vary from this norm, and that a given anthrope or organization may act more or less in accord with his or her alignment from day to day.</p>

<p><em>Note: The names for the alignments given below were specified by a previous version of the source material. Monsters Masters & Mobsters retains these names at our own choosing.</em></p>

<h3><a href="/resources/dd/integrity">Path of Integrity (Lawful Good)</a></h3>

<p>A lawful good anthrope acts as a good anthrope is expected or required to act. He combines a commitment to oppose evil with the discipline to fight relentlessly. He tells the truth, keeps his word, helps those in need, and speaks out against injustice. A lawful good anthrope hates to see the guilty go unpunished.</p>

<p>A lawful good organization places the welfare of its members and the society it serves above all else. It has a strict code of conduct and expects all members to uphold it without exception. It is dedicated to justice, protection of the innocent, and the eradication of evil.

<p>Lawful good is the best alignment you can be because it combines honor and compassion.</p>

<p>Lawful good can be a dangerous alignment when it restricts freedom and criminalizes self-interest.</p>

<h3><a href="/resources/dd/mercy">Path of Mercy (Neutral Good)</a></h3>

<p>A neutral good anthrope does the best that a good anthrope can do. He is devoted to helping others. He works with kings and magistrates but does not feel beholden to them.</p>

<p>A neutral good organization is dedicated to doing good without bias for or against order. It helps those in need and protects the innocent without regard for rules, traditions, or loyalties.</p>

<p>Neutral good is the best alignment you can be because it means doing what is good without bias for or against order.</p>

<p>Neutral good can be a dangerous alignment when it advances mediocrity by limiting the actions of the truly capable.</p>

<h3><a href="/resources/dd/liberty">Path of Liberty (Chaotic Good)</a></h3>

<p>A chaotic good anthrope acts as his conscience directs him with little regard for what others expect of him. He makes his own way, but he's kind and benevolent. He believes in goodness and right but has little use for laws and regulations. He hates it when people try to intimidate others and tell them what to do. He follows his own moral compass, which, although good, may not agree with that of society.</p>

<p>A chaotic good organization values freedom and flexibility but also strives to do good. It champions individual rights and allows its members considerable latitude in how they pursue the organization's goals.</p>

<p>Chaotic good is the best alignment you can be because it combines a good heart with a free spirit.</p>

<p>Chaotic good can be a dangerous alignment when it disrupts the order of society and punishes those who do well for themselves.</p>

<h3><a href="/resources/dd/harmony">Path of Harmony (Lawful Neutral)</a></h3>

<p>A lawful neutral anthrope acts as law, tradition, or a personal code directs her. Order and organization are paramount to her. She may believe in personal order and live by a code or standard, or she may believe in order for all and favor a strong, organized government.</p>

<p>A lawful neutral organization values structure, order, and predictability above all else. It has clear rules, a defined chain of command, and established procedures. It expects all members to uphold its standards without exception.</p>

<p>Lawful neutral is the best alignment you can be because it means you are reliable and honorable without being a zealot.</p>

<p>Lawful neutral can be a dangerous alignment when it seeks to eliminate all freedom, choice, and diversity in society.</p>

<h3><a href="/resources/dd/equity">Path of Equity (True Neutral)</a></h3>

<p>A neutral anthrope does what seems to be a good idea. She doesn't feel strongly one way or the other when it comes to good vs. evil or law vs. chaos. Most neutral anthropes exhibit a lack of conviction or bias rather than a commitment to neutrality. Such a anthrope thinks of good as better than evil-after all, she would rather have good neighbors and rulers than evil ones. Still, she's not personally committed to upholding good in any abstract or universal way.</p>

<p>Some neutral anthropes, on the other hand, commit themselves philosophically to neutrality. They see good, evil, law, and chaos as prejudices and dangerous extremes. They advocate the middle way of neutrality as the best, most balanced road in the long run.</p>

<p>A neutral organization takes a balanced approach to law and chaos as well as good and evil. It values order and freedom equally and seeks to balance the two in its structure and procedures. It helps those in need but does so without bias for or against law or chaos.</p>

<p>Neutral is the best alignment you can be because it means you act naturally, without prejudice or compulsion.</p>

<p>Neutral can be a dangerous alignment when it represents apathy, indifference, and a lack of conviction.</p>

<h3><a href="/resources/dd/autonomy">Path of Autonomy (Chaotic Neutral)</a></h3>

<p>A chaotic neutral anthrope follows his whims. He is an individualist first and last. He values his own liberty but doesn't strive to protect others' freedom. He avoids authority, resents restrictions, and challenges traditions. A chaotic neutral anthrope does not intentionally disrupt organizations as part of a campaign of anarchy. To do so, he would have to be motivated either by good (and a desire to liberate others) or evil (and a desire to make those different from himself suffer). A chaotic neutral anthrope may be unpredictable, but his behavior is not totally random. He is not as likely to jump off a bridge as to cross it.</p>

<p>A chaotic neutral organization values freedom and flexibility above all else. It has few rules, a flexible chain of command, and adaptable procedures. It allows its members considerable latitude in how they pursue the organization's goals.</p>

<p>Chaotic neutral is the best alignment you can be because it represents true freedom from both society's restrictions and a do-gooder's zeal.</p>

<p>Chaotic neutral can be a dangerous alignment when it seeks to eliminate all authority, harmony, and order in society.</p>

<h3><a href="/resources/dd/ascendency">Path of Ascendency (Lawful Evil)</a></h3>

<p>A lawful evil anthrope methodically takes what he wants within the limits of his code of conduct without regard for whom it hurts. He cares about tradition, loyalty, and order but not about freedom, dignity, or life. He plays by the rules but without mercy or compassion. He is comfortable in a hierarchy and would like to rule, but is willing to serve. He condemns others not according to their actions but according to race, religion, homeland, or social rank. He is loath to break laws or promises.</p>

<p>A lawful evil organization is structured and methodical in its pursuit of evil. It has clear rules, a defined chain of command, and established procedures. It expects all members to uphold its standards without exception. It is dedicated to spreading evil, oppressing the innocent, and furthering the organization's power and influence.</p>

<p>The reluctance to break laws or promises comes partly from the lawful nature and partly because the lawful anthrope or organization depends on order to protect themselves from those who oppose them on moral grounds. Some lawful evil villains have particular taboos, such as not killing in cold blood (but having underlings do it) or not letting children come to harm (if it can be helped). They imagine that these compunctions put them above unprincipled villains.</p>

<p>Some lawful evil anthropes and organizations commit themselves to evil with a zeal like that of a crusader committed to good. Beyond being willing to hurt others for their own ends, they take pleasure in spreading evil as an end unto itself. They may also see doing evil as part of a duty to an evil deity or master.</p>

<p>Lawful evil anthropes and organizations consider their alignment to be the best because it combines honor with a dedicated self-interest.</p>

<p>Lawful evil is the most dangerous alignment because it represents methodical, intentional, and frequently successful evil.</p>

<h3><a href="/resources/dd/supremacy">Path of Supremacy (Neutral Evil)</a></h3>

<p>A neutral evil anthrope or organization does whatever they can get away with. They are out for themselves. They shed no tears for those they kill, whether for profit, sport, or convenience. They have no love of order and hold no illusion that following laws, traditions, or codes would make them any better or more noble. On the other hand, they don't have the restless nature or love of conflict that a chaotic evil anthrope or organization would have.</p>

<p>Some neutral evil anthropes and organizations hold up evil as an ideal, committing evil for its own sake. Most often, such villains are devoted to evil deities or secret societies.</p>
    
<p>Neutral evil anthropes and organizations consider their alignment to be the best because they can advance themselves without regard for others.</p>

<p>Neutral evil is the most dangerous alignment because it represents pure evil without honor and without variation.</p>

<h3><a href="/resources/dd/luxury">Path of Luxury (Chaotic Evil)</a></h3>

<p>A chaotic evil anthrope or organization does whatever their greed, hatred, and lust for destruction drive them to do. They are hot-tempered, vicious, arbitrarily violent, and unpredictable. If they are simply out for whatever they can get, they are ruthless and brutal. If they are committed to the spread of evil and chaos, they are even worse. Thankfully, their plans are haphazard, and any groups they join or form are poorly organized. Typically, chaotic evil people can be made to work together only by force, and their leader lasts only as long as they can thwart attempts to topple or assassinate them.</p>

<p>Chaotic evil anthropes and organizations believe their alignment is the best because it combines self-interest and pure freedom.</p>

<p>Chaotic evil is the most dangerous alignment because it represents the destruction not only of beauty and life but also of the order on which beauty and life depend.</p>


